INV-fokik. ADR-0036 Ruling 1 makes `path` the one colon-TOLERANT slot in
{lang}:{path}:{span}:{name}:{kind}. io_boundary._extract_module_hint took
parts[1], so rust:std::env:0-0:var:external_symbol yielded `std`;
_module_matches("std::env","std") is False; and _lookup_named_entry treats a
present-but-MISMATCHED module as a REJECTION rather than a degrade. Every one
of Rust's nine colon-bearing sink modules was refused, silently.

THE ITEM'S PRESCRIBED FIX WOULD HAVE TRADED ONE CORRUPTION FOR ANOTHER, and
only a corpus count showed it. "Route the caller through symbol_path_slot" —
but that chokepoint was RIGHT-anchored (parts[1:-3]) on the grammar's promise
that `name` is colon-free, and the data breaks it. Counted over just, rage and
hypergumbo itself with scripts/measure-symbol-id-colon-conformance.py:

    conformant                        352,254   no-op
    colon PATH   (LEFT-anchor wrong)      740   migration FIXES
    colon NAME   (RIGHT-anchor wrong)     483   migration would REGRESS
    no span token                         584   right-anchor already correct

Span-anchoring is the only parse right on both, and it makes symbol_path_slot
agree with taint._extract_callee_name, which anchored that way all along — the
item names that disagreement between two parsers of one string as "the tell".

FAILS SAFE BY CHOICE. A malformed id has no true path slot; `external` is a
placeholder and degrades to short-name matching plus the F3 gate, whereas
`external:0-0:std::env` is not and rejects everything in silence.

THE PATH FIX ALONE MOVED ZERO, caught by a positive control. With the hint
corrected to std::fs, tagging rust:std::fs:0-0:write:external_symbol still
produced no boundary: io_boundary._extract_callee_name carried the MIRROR
defect in the same file ("everything after the first three fields" assumes a
colon-free PATH) and returned `fs:0-0:write`, missing the lookup before the
hint could matter. New chokepoint ir.symbol_name_slot; io_boundary and taint
both route through it so they cannot drift apart again.

BLAST RADIUS ON A REAL PIPELINE IS ZERO, AND THE ZERO IS EXPLAINED. Both call
sites prefer the structured dst_ref (WI-tihup) and finalize populates it on
every is_resolved=False edge (ADR-0037 ruling 2). All 171 of rage's rust::std
dsts carry dst_ref, so they bypass the legacy parse: 0 gained / 0 lost across
10,656 legacy-parse edges on rage + just, with a positive control proving the
detector fires on the exact shapes. This closes a LATENT hole on the paths
dst_ref does not cover. No recall improvement is claimed; none was observed.

Behavioral evidence (the failure mode is a SILENT drop, so a parser unit test
is not enough): production tag_io_boundaries, same fixture both arms —
PRE hint='std' tagged=0; POST hint='std::fs' tagged=1 primitive=std::fs.write.
Pinned by TestTheEndToEndBoundaryIsActuallyRecovered.

